WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:02.880
- Hey, I'm Matthew, a
Tasty chef and producer,

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:04.370
and today I'm gonna be
showing you how to make

00:00:04.370 --> 00:00:07.001
some impressive, show-stopping dinners.

00:00:07.001 --> 00:00:08.670
(upbeat music)

00:00:08.670 --> 00:00:12.960
I love Beef Wellington because
it's a show-stopping dish.

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:16.020
Beef Wellington is a great test of a chef.

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:18.570
It's one of the most
challenging dishes you can make.

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:21.640
I think this dish is perfect
for a special occasion.

00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:23.720
So, when you sear the
beef, what you really want

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:27.680
is a dark brown, crispy
crust, and this process

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:29.760
of searing is called
the Maillard reaction.

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:32.450
The Maillard reaction is
basically just proteins

00:00:32.450 --> 00:00:35.030
breaking down when they
reach a certain temperature,

00:00:35.030 --> 00:00:36.780
and that gives you your dark crust.

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:40.060
It's important for great flavor
and complexity with beef.

00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:42.340
So, for me, some of the best food will hit

00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:45.290
on many different types of
flavors and notes, and so,

00:00:45.290 --> 00:00:47.470
Beef Wellington kind-of hits
a little bit of everything.

00:00:47.470 --> 00:00:50.700
It is super meaty, and savory, and salty,

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:53.800
and the mushrooms are
very rich and complex,

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:56.540
and then you have a little
bit of spice, some nuance

00:00:56.540 --> 00:00:59.240
from the English mustard
that hits your nose,

00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:02.490
you have like, your super
buttery, flakey puff pastry,

00:01:02.490 --> 00:01:04.720
so when you put a piece of
Beef Wellington in your mouth,

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:07.310
you have a tiny little bit of
all those flavors together,

00:01:07.310 --> 00:01:09.480
and it just mashes together perfectly.

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:12.260
When I was 18, I left my hometown
to go to culinary school,

00:01:12.260 --> 00:01:14.690
which means I'm always
away from my family,

00:01:14.690 --> 00:01:16.880
except for Christmas and New Years,

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:20.100
and when I am home, I always
make our holiday dinners.

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:22.670
Food is an expression
of the heart, and this

00:01:22.670 --> 00:01:25.670
over-the-top, gorgeous Beef
Wellington is a great way

00:01:25.670 --> 00:01:28.270
to show my family just
how much I miss them.

00:01:28.270 --> 00:01:30.830
Creating show-stopping
entrees is all about

00:01:30.830 --> 00:01:33.220
taking the time to cook for
the people you care about.

00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:35.770
The dish has been around
for years and years.

00:01:35.770 --> 00:01:38.410
Gordon Ramsay was kind-of
the person to sort-of

00:01:38.410 --> 00:01:40.100
modernize it, in a lot
of ways, you didn't have

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:43.640
to do the same duck liver
pate, you didn't have to do

00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:45.820
all the old techniques,
you were able to update

00:01:45.820 --> 00:01:47.980
it with stuff like prosciutto and mustard.

00:01:47.980 --> 00:01:50.530
I grew up being in awe
of chefs like Dan Barber,

00:01:50.530 --> 00:01:53.100
Thomas Keller, Wolfgang
Puck, people who really

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:55.860
pushed the boundary of
what the food world is,

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:57.820
as well as food media game changers

00:01:57.820 --> 00:01:59.760
like James Beard and Julia Child.

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:02.300
When I make a dish like
this, it makes me feel

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:04.830
like my idols that I grew up loving would

00:02:04.830 --> 00:02:06.840
be impressed with what
I do, and it gives me

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:08.540
a lot of comfort and
gives me a lot of joy.

00:02:08.540 --> 00:02:10.930
I know if I work hard, one day, I'll be

00:02:10.930 --> 00:02:13.350
a major player in the food
game, and I hope to become

00:02:13.350 --> 00:02:16.370
an inspiration to other
young cooks out there.

00:02:16.370 --> 00:02:18.190
I like to cook these dishes because they

00:02:18.190 --> 00:02:20.110
are a challenge that push me to grow

00:02:20.110 --> 00:02:22.000
as a chef and as a producer at Tasty.

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:24.950
These are just a tiny percentage
of the food that amazing

00:02:24.950 --> 00:02:27.530
chefs I've worked for have
taught me over the years.

00:02:27.530 --> 00:02:30.120
Tasty reaches millions of
people, and I want to teach

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:32.910
them all the classics,
expose them to something new

00:02:32.910 --> 00:02:35.120
and inspire them to be
adventurous in the kitchen.

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:36.160
This is just the beginning.

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:39.710
To me, cooking is so primal,
innate, and hands-on,

00:02:39.710 --> 00:02:42.930
and having great food is
essential to having a great life.

00:02:42.930 --> 00:02:45.730
(upbeat music)

00:02:45.730 --> 00:02:48.170
Chicken Marsala is a simple dish

00:02:48.170 --> 00:02:50.310
that people get easily wowed by.

00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:53.130
This is a dish you can
make on a random Tuesday

00:02:53.130 --> 00:02:56.700
and your family or your
partner will love it.

00:02:56.700 --> 00:02:59.880
There's so many dishes that
are similar to Chicken Marsala.

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:02.490
The techniques in this
dish are used in thousands

00:03:02.490 --> 00:03:04.940
of other recipes, it's
just about applying them

00:03:04.940 --> 00:03:08.040
to this simple dish and
mastering the techniques

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:10.210
so that the final product is delicious.

00:03:10.210 --> 00:03:12.590
What I love about Chicken
Marsala is that is has

00:03:12.590 --> 00:03:16.350
very earthy flavors that
comes from the searing

00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:18.280
of the chicken and from the mushrooms.

00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:20.300
It's nice 'cause you can use Marsala,

00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:23.110
which is a pretty common
wine, but if you don't have

00:03:23.110 --> 00:03:25.860
Marsala, you could really use
any type of deep red wine.

00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:27.340
It's great to like, finish
off a bottle you have

00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:29.590
at home, again, it's like,
a really approachable

00:03:29.590 --> 00:03:32.420
dish that, if you do it
correctly, can impress.

00:03:32.420 --> 00:03:35.520
What's great about Chicken
Marsala is it's very,

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:38.820
like, subtle and buttery,
and it kind-of just

00:03:38.820 --> 00:03:41.380
like, creeps up on you,
like, if you don't cook

00:03:41.380 --> 00:03:44.450
very often, this is an
easy, approachable recipe.

00:03:44.450 --> 00:03:46.130
It's all about the flavor components

00:03:46.130 --> 00:03:47.440
that you're pairing together.

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:49.320
If you hit all the important steps,

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:51.110
you'll have a dish that will impress.

00:03:51.110 --> 00:03:53.870
This is a great show-stopping
weeknight dinner.

00:03:53.870 --> 00:03:56.510
The chicken will be super
succulent and tender,

00:03:56.510 --> 00:03:59.080
it will melt in your
mouth, and the creamy sauce

00:03:59.080 --> 00:04:00.890
should cling to the pasta and chicken.

00:04:00.890 --> 00:04:02.390
Eventually, cooking at home will be

00:04:02.390 --> 00:04:04.660
easier for you than ordering out.

00:04:04.660 --> 00:04:06.810
Also, nothing is better in this world

00:04:06.810 --> 00:04:09.350
than surprising a loved one with a bangin'

00:04:09.350 --> 00:04:11.730
weeknight dinner they didn't expect.

00:04:11.730 --> 00:04:14.700
(upbeat music)

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:16.920
So, with this recipe, I wanna be clear,

00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:19.430
I'm completely aware that
this is not traditional,

00:04:19.430 --> 00:04:23.080
but I thought this would be
a cool twist on a popular

00:04:23.080 --> 00:04:26.520
Italian dish by fusing it
with a classic French dish.

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:29.600
Ratatouille is basically a
tomato and vegetable stew

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:33.020
with lots of French herbs,
and lasagna is a classic

00:04:33.020 --> 00:04:35.470
Italian dish that's been
passed down through so many

00:04:35.470 --> 00:04:37.870
families that it's eventually
come over to America.

00:04:37.870 --> 00:04:40.940
When creating a show-stopping
vegetarian entree,

00:04:40.940 --> 00:04:43.560
I wanted something that's
satisfying and filling

00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:45.940
as a lasagna, and something that had

00:04:45.940 --> 00:04:47.820
as much vegetables as a ratatouille,

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:50.500
and combining these two
was perfect because it took

00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:53.540
the best aspect of a
ratatouille and all of its

00:04:53.540 --> 00:04:56.500
amazing, rich flavors,
and it had a really great

00:04:56.500 --> 00:04:59.290
layering aspect that came
with the Italian lasagna.

00:04:59.290 --> 00:05:01.460
Neither are completely
traditional because they were put

00:05:01.460 --> 00:05:04.310
together, but that's sort-of
what's great about food.

00:05:04.310 --> 00:05:07.580
It's basically a universal
language that we all understand.

00:05:07.580 --> 00:05:09.990
It's more exciting to
be able to learn from

00:05:09.990 --> 00:05:12.270
the best things every country has to offer

00:05:12.270 --> 00:05:14.580
and combine them together
to make something new

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:17.110
and exciting that you
can be thrilled to try.

00:05:17.110 --> 00:05:19.330
When I was a little kid, one of the first

00:05:19.330 --> 00:05:22.750
fusions I ever tried was
combining grilled cheese

00:05:22.750 --> 00:05:24.960
and ham, 'cause I thought
that would be delicious,

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:28.420
and we called them grilled
chams, and now I fully

00:05:28.420 --> 00:05:31.450
realize it was just the grilled
cheese sandwich with ham.

00:05:31.450 --> 00:05:34.630
But now, that same type
of curiosity is what

00:05:34.630 --> 00:05:36.690
I take into creating foods now.

00:05:36.690 --> 00:05:39.760
To me, recipes are just guidelines.

00:05:39.760 --> 00:05:41.810
The person who took the
time to write the recipe,

00:05:41.810 --> 00:05:44.090
if you follow what they do,
you should get the same result

00:05:44.090 --> 00:05:46.530
that they got, but that
doesn't mean that you have to.

00:05:46.530 --> 00:05:49.160
If you want to experiment
and try something new,

00:05:49.160 --> 00:05:51.110
you can make every recipe your own.

00:05:51.110 --> 00:05:53.120
In order to grow as a cook,
you really need to take

00:05:53.120 --> 00:05:55.410
that leap of faith and really experiment.

00:05:55.410 --> 00:05:58.500
Don't be bogged down with
the rules of the world.

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:01.000
If you love two different types of dishes,

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:03.370
combine them together,
make them something new

00:06:03.370 --> 00:06:06.450
and exciting that you can
then share with your friends.

00:06:06.450 --> 00:06:08.620
This is the perfect
show-stopping entree to bring

00:06:08.620 --> 00:06:11.480
to a vegetarian friend's
house, or just if you want

00:06:11.480 --> 00:06:15.160
a lighter alternative to the
rest of these meaty options.

00:06:15.160 --> 00:06:18.470
Speaking of which, here's a
bacon-wrapped prime rib recipe.

00:06:18.470 --> 00:06:20.930
So, this final recipe is a
show-stopper you're gonna

00:06:20.930 --> 00:06:24.350
wanna make to give an entire
crowd a cardiac arrest.

00:06:24.350 --> 00:06:26.840
For the prime rib, we're
doing this reverse-sear

00:06:26.840 --> 00:06:28.940
method, which is the opposite of what we

00:06:28.940 --> 00:06:30.310
did with the Beef Wellington.

00:06:30.310 --> 00:06:32.700
What you're doing is
ensuring that all the meat

00:06:32.700 --> 00:06:35.230
on the inside is cooked
medium-rare, and then

00:06:35.230 --> 00:06:37.990
you're just going to cook
the outside at a high

00:06:37.990 --> 00:06:40.210
heat to get that nice, Maillard reaction

00:06:40.210 --> 00:06:42.170
and browned bacon flavor your want.

00:06:42.170 --> 00:06:44.890
So, the thought behind
this dish was basically,

00:06:44.890 --> 00:06:48.610
I wanted something that
was a complete monstrosity,

00:06:48.610 --> 00:06:51.550
but you were intrigued
nonetheless, and it tasted great.

00:06:51.550 --> 00:06:53.930
Every once in a while,
people just want something

00:06:53.930 --> 00:06:56.180
that's gonna blow their mind, and that was

00:06:56.180 --> 00:06:58.100
the goal of making this prime rib.

00:06:58.100 --> 00:07:01.030
It never fails to shock me how easily

00:07:01.030 --> 00:07:03.410
people are impressed with good food.

00:07:03.410 --> 00:07:06.300
You can make a simple dish
that just tastes great

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:08.640
and people act like you
reinvented the wheel.

00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:11.050
So, when I walk in with my food offering,

00:07:11.050 --> 00:07:13.730
I really want to blow people's socks off.

00:07:13.730 --> 00:07:15.990
I want them to talk about the food I made

00:07:15.990 --> 00:07:18.530
for them to their friends,
and nothing impresses

00:07:18.530 --> 00:07:21.380
a crowd more than a huge piece of meat.

00:07:21.380 --> 00:07:23.320
Something like this
bacon-wrapped prime rib

00:07:23.320 --> 00:07:26.170
is perfect for when you want
to be the winner at a party,

00:07:26.170 --> 00:07:29.050
and I guess, at the end of
the day, that's why we all

00:07:29.050 --> 00:07:32.410
love show-stopping food,
because we all love that moment

00:07:32.410 --> 00:07:35.510
of shock and awe on someone
else's face when they

00:07:35.510 --> 00:07:38.710
just can't wait to dive into
the food you cooked for them.

00:07:38.710 --> 00:07:41.210
So, growing up, my family
always cooked in the kitchen,

00:07:41.210 --> 00:07:43.140
I spent every waking
moment in the kitchen,

00:07:43.140 --> 00:07:45.550
and I would watch food
television all the time,

00:07:45.550 --> 00:07:48.380
and I just knew I wanted to
work in food my whole life.

00:07:48.380 --> 00:07:50.830
And basically, knowing what you want to do

00:07:50.830 --> 00:07:53.850
at a young age, it makes
you become a huge planner.

00:07:53.850 --> 00:07:56.130
It's never not cool to be the hard worker.

00:07:56.130 --> 00:07:59.030
It's never not respected to
be someone that works hard.

00:07:59.030 --> 00:08:01.600
This is what I wanted to do with my life.

00:08:01.600 --> 00:08:03.900
So, I love when I make food that I know

00:08:03.900 --> 00:08:06.480
I put time, and care, and love into.

00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:08.210
When I pass if off to
someone else and I just

00:08:08.210 --> 00:08:10.770
see their face light up,
it brings me a lot of joy.

00:08:10.770 --> 00:08:13.540
This is why every meal
I try and cook myself,

00:08:13.540 --> 00:08:15.870
why I work my butt off
every day, and why I try

00:08:15.870 --> 00:08:18.820
to make everything I do
be the best it can be.

00:08:18.820 --> 00:08:21.270
My entire life revolves around cooking

00:08:21.270 --> 00:08:23.460
and I love it because
it allows me to connect

00:08:23.460 --> 00:08:26.660
with other people and
tell stories through food.

00:08:26.660 --> 00:08:27.940
Everyone has some favorite food

00:08:27.940 --> 00:08:29.510
memories, tell me some of yours.

00:08:29.510 --> 00:08:31.131
- [Tasty Guy] Oh, yes.

00:08:31.131 --> 00:08:33.714
(upbeat music)

